      "tag": "ANALYSIS",
      "tagCls": "analysis",
      "type": "neutral",
      "author": "ML",
      "authorName": "Marcus Leigh",
      "headline": "Florida: The race that could define the night",
      "body": "With <strong>74% reporting</strong> and Morrison holding a slim R+4.4 lead, Florida remains the pivotal test for Democrats. The outstanding precincts are concentrated in Miami-Dade and Broward — historically Democratic strongholds. If late urban returns mirror 2022 patterns, Ramos has a path to close the gap."
    },
    {
      "time": "23:18",
      "tag": "PROJECTION",
      "tagCls": "projection",
      "type": "dem-post",
      "author": "DN",
      "authorName": "Dan Norton",
      "headline": "CBS News projects: New York goes to Webb (D)",
      "body": "With <strong>87% of precincts reporting</strong>, CBS News projects Democrat Marcus Webb wins the New York Governor's race. Webb built strong margins in New York City's outer boroughs, while holding competitive in key suburban counties including Nassau and Westchester."
    },
    {
      "time": "23:11",
      "tag": "CALIFORNIA",
      "tagCls": "dem",
      "type": "dem-post",
      "author": "PV",
      "authorName": "Patricia Vega",
      "headline": "Chen dominates Los Angeles — 93% in, D+24",
      "body": "Los Angeles County continues to deliver the margins Amara Chen needs. With 93% of precincts reporting, Chen leads by <strong>24 points</strong> in the state's most populous county. San Diego and Orange County have shown modest Republican gains but not enough to offset the LA advantage."
    },
    {
      "time": "23:07",
      "tag": "FLORIDA",
      "tagCls": "rep",
      "type": "rep-post",
      "author": "PV",
      "authorName": "Patricia Vega",
      "headline": "Florida mail ballots tell a different story for Ramos",
      "body": "CBS News analysis finds that mail ballots counted tonight in Florida favor Democrat Elena Ramos by <strong>11 points</strong> over in-person voting. The question is volume — if enough mail ballots remain uncounted, Ramos could cut into Morrison's current lead. Election officials in Palm Beach say counting continues past midnight."
    },
    {
      "time": "23:02",
      "tag": "PROJECTION",
      "tagCls": "projection",
      "type": "dem-post",
      "author": "DN",
      "authorName": "Dan Norton",
      "headline": "CBS News projects: Amara Chen wins California",
      "body": "CBS News projects Democrat <strong>Amara Chen wins the California Governor's race</strong>, claiming 54 electoral votes in the presidential race. Chen's dominant performance in Southern California proved insurmountable for Republican Curtis Vance. The call comes with 91% of precincts reporting and Chen leading 62.1% to 37.9%."
    },
    {
      "time": "22:57",
      "tag": "COLORADO",
      "tagCls": "rep",
      "type": "rep-post",
      "author": "ML",
      "authorName": "Marcus Leigh",
      "headline": "El Paso County shifts right — Colorado tightening",
      "body": "Republican Tom Bradley has outperformed expectations in El Paso County, running <strong>R+6 compared to 2022</strong>. Mitchell's campaign had hoped to hold Bradley under R+4 there. Weld County also showing stronger Republican numbers. The race has tightened to within 3 points with 63% reporting statewide."
    },
    {
      "time": "22:51",
      "tag": "BREAKING",
      "tagCls": "breaking",
      "type": "breaking",
      "author": "DN",
      "authorName": "Dan Norton",
      "headline": "Senate balance: Republicans hold 48-47 edge with 5 races uncalled",
      "body": "As results continue to come in, Republicans hold a narrow 48-47 advantage in Senate seats with five races still uncalled — Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, Nevada, Georgia, and Arizona. Democrats need to sweep all five to gain control; Republicans need only two. The Pennsylvania race, currently showing <strong>D+1.6</strong>, is seen as the most critical contest."
    },
    {
      "time": "22:44",
      "tag": "ANALYSIS",
      "tagCls": "analysis",
      "type": "neutral",
      "author": "ML",
      "authorName": "Marcus Leigh",
      "headline": "Turnout surge in Northeast could reshape Senate math",
      "body": "Early turnout data from New York and Pennsylvania shows Democratic-leaning urban areas running significantly higher than 2022 levels. In Manhattan, Brooklyn, and Queens, turnout is up <strong>4-6 points</strong>. This pattern, if sustained through final counts, could shift the Senate picture toward Democrats — particularly in the closely watched Pennsylvania contest."
    }
